WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.575
Array bounds defects often appear only at edge sizes and failure paths.

2
00:00:04.575 --> 00:00:12.201
Tests should cover zero elements, one element, expected maximums, just-below and just-above limits, empty input, truncated input, inconsistent

3
00:00:12.201 --> 00:00:16.395
lengths, and indexes near the beginning and end of a range.

4
00:00:16.395 --> 00:00:20.971
AddressSanitizer can help reveal memory access defects while tests exercise the code.

5
00:00:20.971 --> 00:00:25.165
UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer can reveal behavior that the program should not rely on.

6
00:00:25.165 --> 00:00:33.172
These tools are most useful in development and continuous integration, where the extra checks can run before a defect reaches users.

7
00:00:33.172 --> 00:00:36.222
Compiler warnings and static diagnostics add another layer.

8
00:00:36.222 --> 00:00:43.085
Warnings can flag suspicious conversions, signed and unsigned comparisons, array indexing concerns, unreachable conditions, and other code patterns

9
00:00:43.085 --> 00:00:43.848
worth review.

10
00:00:43.848 --> 00:00:49.948
A useful warning policy explains which findings are required to fix and how exceptions are documented.

11
00:00:49.948 --> 00:00:55.286
Hardened library modes and debug iterators can catch invalid range assumptions in some builds.

12
00:00:55.286 --> 00:01:04.056
They are not a substitute for safe design, and they may not match production performance settings, but they give teams valuable feedback when

13
00:01:04.056 --> 00:01:06.343
integrated into test and CI pipelines.

14
00:01:06.343 --> 00:01:08.250
Tools need process around them.

15
00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:17.019
A sanitizer report, warning, or boundary test failure should become a tracked engineering defect with an owner, fix, regression test, and follow-up

16
00:01:17.019 --> 00:01:17.401
review.

17
00:01:17.401 --> 00:01:23.120
Bounds-safety checks become stronger when they are repeatable, visible, and part of normal delivery.
